window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-cec78ec9a80a4b51"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"see-which-campaigns-bring-in-leads\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">查看哪些营销活动带来了潜在客户</h1> <p>按营销活动、渠道和周比较花费、潜在客户数量及每条潜在客户的成本。</p> <p>将每周营销活动导出数据转化为仪表板，让团队比较潜在客户数量和成本、按渠道筛选变化，并查看汇总数据背后的明细行。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>需要比较不同渠道潜在客户数量和每条潜在客户成本的营销人员。</li> <li>需要审核广告平台和 CRM 报告每周导出数据的团队。</li> <li>想用可交互的仪表板替代静态周报的团队。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">起步提示词</h2> <p>查看哪些营销活动带来了潜在客户</p> <p>建议推理级别：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">使用 @Sites，为[team]（团队）基于[attached spreadsheet or exact source link and tab]（附件电子表格或准确的数据源链接及工作表标签）构建营销活动仪表板。\n\n报告背景：\n- 审核问题：[what the team wants to understand from this report]（团队希望通过报告了解什么）。\n- 时间范围：[start and end dates]（开始和结束日期）。\n- 币种：[currency]（币种）。\n- 周起始日和时区：[definition]（定义）。\n- 潜在客户定义和归因规则：[team definition]（团队定义）。\n- 如有需要，补充数据源和营销活动 ID 映射：[file or none]（文件或无）。\n\n先检查数据源，并展示列名、行数、日期范围和示例行。预期每行代表每个营销活动在某一周的数据，包含营销活动 ID 或名称、渠道、花费和潜在客户数量。标出重复项、缺失值、混合币种和无法匹配的营销活动 ID。对不明确的映射提出问题；不要静默丢弃行或编造数据。\n\n展示总花费、潜在客户总数和每条潜在客户的成本。添加营销活动、渠道和周筛选器、每周趋势图以及营销活动对比表。允许评审者查看选中营销活动汇总数据背后的源数据行。所有卡片、图表和表格必须使用同一组筛选后数据。标注币种、报告时间范围、数据源和任何被排除的行。如果所提供日期表明某周尚未结束，请标出这是部分周数据。\n\n每条潜在客户的成本应按所选行的总花费除以潜在客户总数计算，不能对各行比例取平均。潜在客户数量为零时显示 N/A；将缺失值与零区分开；没有匹配行时显示空状态。除非我提供换算规则，否则不同币种的数据应分开显示。\n\n从所提供数据的已保存副本开始。说明如何用新导出文件替换数据，同时避免重复导入重叠周的数据。除非已配置并测试受支持的连接，否则不要称其为实时数据。不要编辑源报告或营销预算，也不要仅凭潜在客户数量推断收入或潜在客户质量。\n\n对照源数据核查合计。使用单独标记的示例数据进行测试：花费 $1,000 并获得 25 条潜在客户，加上花费 $500 并获得 5 条潜在客户，结果应为花费 $1,500、获得 30 条潜在客户、每条潜在客户成本 $50；不要把这些示例行加入真实报告。还要测试潜在客户数量为零、缺失值、重复行和组合筛选器。检查手机布局。将仪表板设为仅对工作区开放，并在发布前展示已保存的预览、验证结果和未解决的数据问题。\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-e0f092cc982945c2?anchor=build-a-site-with-connected-data\">连接数据源</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/codex/sites/publishing-and-sharing#review-before-you-share\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">分享前检查（需联网）</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"before-you-start\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">开始之前</h2> <p>把每周营销活动导出数据转换为可供团队在复盘时使用的仪表板。将花费、潜在客户数量和每条潜在客户的成本放在一起显示，让同事可以比较不同营销活动、缩小到特定渠道或周，并打开结果背后的明细行。</p> <p>先确定仪表板要回答的问题。你可能想找出花费增加、但潜在客户数量没有相应增加的营销活动；也可能想了解变化来自单个营销活动，还是整个渠道。这个问题有助于你选择合适的筛选器和对比方式。</p> <p>导出所要审核时间范围内的电子表格。每行记录一个营销活动的一周数据；如果名称可能变化，请加入稳定的营销活动 ID。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>字段</th> <th>示例值</th> <th>构建前检查</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>周起始日期</td> <td>2026-10-05</td> <td>所有来源使用相同的周起始规则和时区</td> </tr> <tr> <td>营销活动 ID 和名称</td> <td>SEARCH-01，Product demo</td> <td>各数据源中的 ID 都指向同一营销活动</td> </tr> <tr> <td>渠道</td> <td>付费搜索</td> <td>渠道名称保持一致</td> </tr> <tr> <td>花费和币种</td> <td>1000，USD</td> <td>数据只使用一种币种，或者分币种组显示</td> </tr> <tr> <td>潜在客户</td> <td>25</td> <td>所有营销活动都使用相同的潜在客户定义</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>提供团队的潜在客户定义和报告时间范围。如果花费来自广告报告，而潜在客户来自客户关系管理（CRM）报告，还要提供双方商定的营销活动映射和归因规则。缺少这些信息时，网站无法可靠地关联两份报告。</p> <p><a id=\"connect-your-sources\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">连接资料来源</h2> <ol> <li>在构建对话中附上 CSV 或电子表格。如果工作表位于已连接的应用中，请询问 ChatGPT 当前有哪些连接可以读取它，然后提供文件链接、工作表名称，以及相关区域或列名称，并完成它要求的连接步骤。例如，如果可用连接能够读取工作表，请说明 Google Drive 中“Weekly campaign results”文件、“Campaigns”工作表以及 A–F 列。否则，请将该工作表导出为 CSV 并作为附件提供。</li> <li>构建之前，请让 ChatGPT 显示列名、报告日期、行数和几条示例记录。找出空值、重复的“营销活动—周”记录以及无法匹配的营销活动 ID，并确认如何处理。</li> <li>从导出数据的已保存副本开始，并标注其来源和报告时间范围。要更新数据，请在同一对话中提供新文件，并说明是用新文件替换数据集，还是添加新的周数据。合并多个导出文件之前，请检查是否有重叠。</li> <li>如果访客打开网站时需要加载当前数据，请让 ChatGPT 验证它能否连接到确切的工作表或报告源。将网站设为仅对工作区开放，并使用队友自己的连接进行测试。参阅<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections#build-a-site-with-connected-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">数据与连接</a>（官网章节，需联网） <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">本地页面（本地尚未收录）</a>。</li> </ol> <p>数据源链接或上传的导出文件不会自动创建实时连接。如果要配置已连接的数据版本，请请求添加手动 <strong>Refresh（刷新）</strong>操作，并显示最近一次成功刷新的时间。如果访问失败，应显示错误，或显示标注了刷新时间的旧结果；不要用看起来像“没有活动”的空报告代替。</p> <p><a id=\"how-to-use-it\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">使用方法</h2> <ol> <li>对照导出文件核对合计</li> </ol> <p>使用起始提示词，然后将未筛选的合计与源数据比较。依赖汇总卡片之前，请自行核对一小部分数据。例如，下面的假设性营销活动展示了仪表板应如何合并结果：</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>示例营销活动</th> <th>花费</th> <th>潜在客户</th> <th>每条潜在客户成本</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Product demo：搜索</td> <td>$1,000</td> <td>25</td> <td>$40</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Product demo：社交媒体</td> <td>$500</td> <td>5</td> <td>$100</td> </tr> <tr> <td>合计</td> <td>$1,500</td> <td>30</td> <td>$50</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>合计的每条潜在客户成本为 $1,500 除以 30 条，即 $50。对 $40 和 $100 取平均会错误地得到 $70，因为两个营销活动带来的潜在客户数量不同。让 ChatGPT 展示总数的计算方式，然后对照源数据行检查。</p> <p>测试潜在客户数量为零的行、缺失值，以及没有任何匹配行的筛选条件。卡片、图表和表格应始终使用同一组筛选结果，并将缺失数据与零区分开。</p> <ol> <li>比较数量、成本和每周变化</li> </ol> <p>为要比较的营销活动选择相同的报告时间范围。将潜在客户数量与每条潜在客户的成本一起查看：某个营销活动的单位成本可能很低，但它对潜在客户总数的贡献也可能很少。然后筛选某个渠道，并检查每周趋势，找出变化从何时开始。</p> <p>使用营销活动表格判断哪些项目需要进一步调查。如果花费增加而潜在客户数量保持不变，请检查该时段的源报告和营销活动更改。如果某一周的数据看起来异常偏低，先确认该周是否完整，以及是否包含所有行。单靠潜在客户数量无法证明潜在客户质量、收入或变化的原因。</p> <ol> <li>为每周复盘准备最新数据</li> </ol> <p>检查受众设置，并为团队发布仪表板。每次会议前替换导出文件，或刷新已连接的数据源，然后核对报告时间范围、合计和被排除的行。在会议中，针对有人提出疑问的结果应用筛选器，并追溯到源数据。确定一项具体的后续工作，例如检查某个营销活动的潜在客户定义，或审核近期创意素材的更改。不要把预算变更纳入仪表板的数据汇报流程。</p> <p><a id=\"take-it-further\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">进一步优化</h2> <p><a id=\"refresh-from-a-connected-spreadsheet\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_9\">从已连接的电子表格刷新数据</h3> <p>使用受支持的只读连接替代反复上传文件，并显示仪表板最近一次成功刷新的时间。</p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Check whether an available connection can read [spreadsheet link], tab [tab name], with columns [column names]. If supported, add a manual Refresh action using each visitor's own connection. Validate the new rows before replacing displayed results, preserve the current filters, and show the source, reporting period, and last successful refresh. Handle denied access and missing columns visibly. Keep this read-only; don't edit the sheet or change campaign budgets. If unsupported, retain the file-based update workflow.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">检查可用的连接能否读取 [spreadsheet link] 中的 [tab name] 工作表，以及其中的 [column names] 列。如果支持，添加一个手动 Refresh（刷新）操作，并使用每位访客自己的连接。替换当前显示结果前，先验证新数据行；保留当前筛选条件，并显示来源、报告期间和上次成功刷新的时间。对于访问被拒绝或缺少列的情况，应明确显示提示。保持只读；不要编辑工作表，也不要更改广告活动预算。如果不支持，继续保留通过文件更新的工作流程。</div></div> <p><a id=\"compare-consecutive-weeks\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_10\">比较连续两周的数据</h3> <p>使用两段时间一致的筛选和合计，显示与前一个自然周相比发生了哪些变化。</p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Compare the selected week with the previous calendar week for spend, leads, and cost per lead, using the same campaign and channel filters. Show absolute changes and the two date ranges. Calculate each period's cost per lead from its total spend and leads. Label missing prior-week data and undefined ratios as unavailable rather than zero. Check the results against a small sample before saving the update.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">在保持广告活动和渠道筛选条件一致的情况下，对比选定周与上一个日历周的支出、潜在客户数量和每条潜在客户成本。显示绝对变化值以及两个日期范围。用每个期间的总支出和潜在客户总数计算该期间的每条潜在客户成本。将上周数据缺失和比率无定义的情况标为不可用，不要记为零。保存更新前，先用一小部分样本核对结果。</div></div>","en":"<h1 id=\"see-which-campaigns-bring-in-leads\">See which campaigns bring in leads</h1> <p>Compare spend, lead counts, and cost per lead by campaign, channel, and week.</p> <p>Turn weekly campaign exports into a dashboard where your team can compare lead volume and cost, filter a change by channel, and inspect the rows behind the totals.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>Marketers comparing lead volume and cost per lead across channels.</li> <li>Teams reviewing weekly exports from ad platforms and CRM reports.</li> <li>Teams replacing a static weekly report with a dashboard people can explore.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>See which campaigns bring in leads</p> <p>Suggested reasoning effort：<code>medium</code></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Use @Sites to build a campaign dashboard for [team] from [attached spreadsheet or exact source link and tab].\n\nReporting context:\n- Review question: [what the team wants to understand from this report].\n- Period: [start and end dates].\n- Currency: [currency].\n- Week boundary and time zone: [definition].\n- Lead definition and attribution rule: [team definition].\n- Additional source and campaign-ID mapping, if needed: [file or none].\n\nFirst inspect the source and show the columns, row count, date range, and sample rows. Expect one row per campaign per week with campaign ID or name, channel, spend, and leads. Flag duplicates, missing values, mixed currencies, and unmatched campaign IDs. Ask how to resolve ambiguous mappings; don't silently drop rows or invent data.\n\nShow total spend, total leads, and cost per lead. Add campaign, channel, and week filters, a weekly trend chart, and a campaign comparison table. Let a reviewer inspect the source rows behind a selected campaign's totals. Make every card, chart, and table use the same filtered rows. Label currency, reporting period, source, and any excluded rows. Flag a partial reporting week when the supplied dates establish that it is incomplete.\n\nCalculate cost per lead as total spend divided by total leads for the selected rows, never the average of row-level ratios. Show N/A for zero-lead ratios, distinguish missing values from zero, and show an empty state when no rows match. Keep different currencies separate unless I supply a conversion rule.\n\nStart with a saved copy of the supplied data. Explain how to replace it with a new export without duplicating overlapping weeks. Don't describe it as live unless a supported connection is configured and tested. Don't edit source reports or campaign budgets, or infer revenue or lead quality from lead counts.\n\nCheck totals against the source. Use a separate, labeled sample to test that $1,000 with 25 leads plus $500 with five leads gives $1,500 spend, 30 leads, and $50 cost per lead; don't add these sample rows to the real report. Also test zero leads, missing values, duplicate rows, and combined filters. Check the mobile layout. Keep the dashboard private to our workspace and show a saved preview, validation results, and unresolved data questions before publishing.\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-e0f092cc982945c2?anchor=build-a-site-with-connected-data\">Connect a data source</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/codex/sites/publishing-and-sharing#review-before-you-share\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Review before sharing (online)</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"before-you-start\">Before you start</h2> <p>Turn weekly campaign exports into a dashboard your team can explore during a review. Show spend, lead volume, and cost per lead together so colleagues can compare campaigns, narrow to a channel or week, and open the rows behind a result.</p> <p>Choose the question the dashboard should help answer. You might want to find campaigns where spend increased without a similar increase in leads, or see whether a change came from one campaign or an entire channel. That question helps you choose useful filters and comparisons.</p> <p>Export a spreadsheet for the period you want to review. Use one row per campaign per week and include a stable campaign ID if names can change.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Field</th> <th>Sample value</th> <th>Check before building</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Week start</td> <td>2026-10-05</td> <td>All sources use the same week boundary and time zone</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Campaign ID and name</td> <td>SEARCH-01, Product demo</td> <td>The ID identifies the same campaign in each source</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Channel</td> <td>Paid search</td> <td>Channel names are consistent</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Spend and currency</td> <td>1000, USD</td> <td>Values use one currency or remain in separate currency groups</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Leads</td> <td>25</td> <td>Every campaign uses the same lead definition</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Supply your team's lead definition and the reporting period. If spend comes from an advertising report and leads come from a customer relationship management (CRM) report, also provide the agreed campaign mapping and attribution rule. Without these, the site can't reliably connect the two reports.</p> <h2 id=\"connect-your-sources\">Connect your sources</h2> <ol> <li>Attach a CSV or spreadsheet in the build conversation. For a sheet in a connected app, ask ChatGPT which connections can read it, then provide the file link, tab name, and relevant range or column names. Complete any connection step it requests. For example, name the “Weekly campaign results” file in Google Drive, the “Campaigns” tab, and columns A–F, if an available connection can read that sheet. Otherwise, export the tab as a CSV and attach it.</li> <li>Ask ChatGPT to show the columns, reporting dates, row count, and a few sample rows before building. Identify blank values, duplicate campaign-week rows, and unmatched campaign IDs. Confirm how to handle them.</li> <li>Start with a saved copy of the export. Label it with its source and reporting period. To update it, provide a fresh file in the same conversation and say whether it replaces the dataset or adds new weeks. Check for overlap before combining exports.</li> <li>For data that loads when someone opens the site, ask ChatGPT to verify a supported read connection to the exact sheet or reporting source. Keep the site private to your workspace and test with a teammate's connection. See <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections#build-a-site-with-connected-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">data and connections</a> (official section, online) <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/sites/data-and-connections\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local page (not in this local snapshot)</a>.</li> </ol> <p>A source link or uploaded export doesn't create a live connection. For a connected version, request a manual Refresh action and a visible last-successful-refresh time. If access fails, show an error or previous results labeled with their refresh time rather than an empty report that looks like zero activity.</p> <h2 id=\"how-to-use-it\">How to use it</h2> <ol> <li>Reconcile the totals with your export</li> </ol> <p>Use the starter prompt, then compare unfiltered totals with the source. Check a small selection yourself before relying on the summary cards. For example, these hypothetical campaigns illustrate how the dashboard should combine results:</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Sample campaign</th> <th>Spend</th> <th>Leads</th> <th>Cost per lead</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Product demo: Search</td> <td>$1,000</td> <td>25</td> <td>$40</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Product demo: Social</td> <td>$500</td> <td>5</td> <td>$100</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Combined</td> <td>$1,500</td> <td>30</td> <td>$50</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Combined cost per lead is $1,500 divided by 30 leads, or $50. Averaging $40 and $100 would incorrectly give $70 because the campaigns generated different numbers of leads. Ask ChatGPT to show how it calculates the total, then check it against your source rows.</p> <p>Test a zero-lead row, a missing value, and a filter with no matching rows. The cards, chart, and table should all use the same selection and distinguish missing data from zero.</p> <ol> <li>Compare volume, cost, and weekly changes</li> </ol> <p>Choose the same reporting period for the campaigns you want to compare. Look at lead counts alongside cost per lead: a campaign can have a low unit cost while contributing only a small share of total leads. Then filter to a channel and inspect the weekly trend to find where a change began.</p> <p>Use the campaign table to identify what needs a closer look. If spend rose but leads stayed flat, check the source report and campaign changes for that period. If one week looks unusually low, first check whether the week is complete and all rows were included. Lead counts alone don't establish lead quality, revenue, or what caused a change.</p> <ol> <li>Bring current data to the weekly review</li> </ol> <p>Review the audience and publish the dashboard for your team. Before each meeting, replace the export or refresh the connected source, then verify the reporting period, totals, and any excluded rows. In the meeting, filter to a result someone has a question about and trace it back to the source. Leave with a specific follow-up, such as checking a campaign's lead definition or reviewing a recent creative change. Keep budget changes outside the dashboard's reporting workflow.</p> <h2 id=\"take-it-further\">Take it further</h2> <h3 id=\"refresh-from-a-connected-spreadsheet\">Refresh from a connected spreadsheet</h3> <p>Replace repeated file uploads with a supported read connection that shows when the dashboard last refreshed successfully.</p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Check whether an available connection can read [spreadsheet link], tab [tab name], with columns [column names]. If supported, add a manual Refresh action using each visitor's own connection. Validate the new rows before replacing displayed results, preserve the current filters, and show the source, reporting period, and last successful refresh. Handle denied access and missing columns visibly. Keep this read-only; don't edit the sheet or change campaign budgets. If unsupported, retain the file-based update workflow.\n</code></pre> <h3 id=\"compare-consecutive-weeks\">Compare consecutive weeks</h3> <p>Show what changed from the previous calendar week, using consistent filters and totals for both periods.</p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Compare the selected week with the previous calendar week for spend, leads, and cost per lead, using the same campaign and channel filters. Show absolute changes and the two date ranges. Calculate each period's cost per lead from its total spend and leads. Label missing prior-week data and undefined ratios as unavailable rather than zero. Check the results against a small sample before saving the update.\n</code></pre>"};
